cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "One Budget: Why Correlation Magnitude Cannot Be a Shared Clock"
abstract: >-
  A synthesis of two independently-run hardware programmes, with two dedicated
  hardware runs of its own. Both programmes are shown to measure the same law --
  coherence surviving as a product of half-angle state overlaps, one factor per
  party that has acquired distinguishing information -- and every correlation
  magnitude either measured is drawn on a single conserved unit, spent twice
  over: once between a subsystem's local coherence and its entanglement with a
  partner (Jakob-Bergou complementarity, 2003), once between its entanglement
  with one partner and with another (Coffman-Kundu-Wootters monogamy, 2000).
  Both totals are exactly 1, and depolarizing noise never pushes either above
  it, so the accounting is stronger on hardware than on paper. The consequence:
  a Page-Wootters clock is a good clock in proportion to the entanglement it
  spends on its own system, so any magnitude offered as a shared time between
  two good clocks is already spent, and no quantity on this budget can be a
  shared clock. Both trade-offs were then measured as continuous hardware
  curves on ibm_marrakesh -- IBM-11 (53 circuits, 9/9 pre-registered gates,
  witness versus CHSH at r = -0.9573, with the witness-maximising state
  producing no Bell violation at all) and IBM-12 (135 circuits, 6/6 gates,
  clock quality versus synchronisability at r = -0.9833, both endpoints
  reaching exactly zero). The identities are standard and are attributed as
  such; the measured curves are the contribution. Also includes the two exact
  structural nulls, the independent convergence with a fourth line, and the
  derived Holevo and relativistic storage/throughput bounds that answer the
  engineering questions the line began with. The open question is stated
  explicitly: the Aharonov-Anandan geometric phase is not a magnitude and is
  reparameterisation-invariant, which is the invariance a shared time would
  require; whether it is also on the budget is untested, and a run designed to
  settle it in either direction is specified.
type: software
